        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nAlthough useful, unfortunately it doesn't quite fit in the module's name (as those are not trailing spaces). There are several approaches to add this kind of functionality to user's config:\n- Set \"lead\" and \"tab\" fields of 'listchars' option. This will highlight *all* leading spaces (i.e. spaces from start of line until the first non-whitespace character) regardless of whether they precede tab or not. But if you use only tabs, then this should be okay. So something like this:\n    ```lua\n    vim.o.list = true\n    vim.o.listchars = 'lead:·,tab:> '\n    ```\n- Create a custom helper to add highlighting specifically for spaces that precede tabs. This uses `vim.fn.matchadd()` which is also what 'mini.trailspace' uses:\n    ```lua\n    local windows_match_id = {}\n    local add_pretab_space_hl = function()\n      -- Check if there is already exists one to not create many of them\n      local win_id = vim.api.nvim_get_current_win()\n      local match_id = windows_match_id[win_id]\n      if vim.iter(vim.fn.getmatches()):any(function(m) return m.id == match_id end) then return end\n\n      -- Add match for spaces preceding tabs. This will highlight them in any part\n      -- of line. Adjust pattern to meet the requirements.\n      windows_match_id[win_id] = vim.fn.matchadd('MiniTrailspace', [[ \\+\\ze\\t]])\n    end\n\n    add_pretab_space_hl()\n    vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('WinEnter', { callback = add_pretab_space_hl })\n    ```\n\nClosing as not planned.",
            "created_at": "2025-02-14T13:41:55Z",
